@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace RingTheory

/-- A single étale algebra trivial modulo J admits exactly one map to a henselian pair
extending a morphism of pairs.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0A02, final paragraph, using Tags 09XI, 02FL, and 09XF.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-henselization-stage-map)

Proof: Give A its B-algebra structure through φ and C its structure through f. Base
change C to D = A ⊗[B] C. This is étale over A. The isomorphism B/J ≃ C/JC base changes
to A/I ≃ D/ID because φ(J) is contained in I. Its inverse supplies a section modulo I.
The henselian factorization criterion gives the Jacobson-radical condition and Gabber's
root condition; section lifting then gives an A-algebra section D → A. Restriction
along C → D gives the required map. For uniqueness, every extension induces an
A-algebra section of D. Split the multiplication section of the étale algebra
D ⊗[A] D by an idempotent e generating its diagonal factor. Any section pair sends e
to an idempotent of A. Modulo I both sections coincide, since A/I → D/ID is surjective,
so this idempotent is 1 modulo I. Idempotents are determined modulo an ideal in the
Jacobson radical, hence it equals 1. The kernel of multiplication is generated by
1-e, so the section pair kills x ⊗ 1 - 1 ⊗ x for every x. The sections therefore
agree, and so do their restrictions to C. This works also for the zero ring: the
radical condition with I = ⊤ forces A to be zero. -/
theorem existsUnique_henselization_stage_map
    (B : Type u) [CommRing B] (J : Ideal B)
    (C : Type u) [CommRing C] (f : B →+* C)
    (hf : AlgebraicGeometry.Etale (AlgebraicGeometry.Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom f)))
    (hquot : Function.Bijective
      (Ideal.quotientMap (J.map f) f (Ideal.le_comap_map)))
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] (I : Ideal A) [HenselianRing A I]
    (φ : B →+* A) (hφ : J.map φ ≤ I) :
    ∃! τ : C →+* A, τ.comp f = φ := by
  sorry

end RingTheory
